{
 "MonitoringProgramDescription": "Tree mortality is the forest dying faster than it grows back. Drought weakens trees, and bark beetles and other insects kill the weakened ones, often across whole hillsides. Dense stands, where trees compete for water, are hit hardest.\n\nThe 2023 survey found fir engraver beetles killing California red fir across the Basin's upper slopes, with Jeffrey pine beetle and mountain pine beetle in smaller patches. Dead trees add to the fuel that can carry severe fire, which ties tree mortality to both stand density and fire dynamics.",
 "MonitoringApproach": "The USDA Forest Service aerial detection survey is flown over California's forests each summer. Surveyors sketch each area of recent damage from the air and record the host tree, the agent that killed it, the share of trees affected, and an estimate of trees killed.\n\nThis page reads the 2023 survey: the damage areas within or touching the TRPA boundary. Areas that cross the boundary are counted whole, so the totals run higher than the Basin alone. Each year's survey added to the service builds the trend.",
